# Headings

@lede
Six levels of `#`-prefixed headings. No setext form. No HTML escape hatch.
@end

## The six levels

```brief
# Heading level 1
## Heading level 2
### Heading level 3
#### Heading level 4
##### Heading level 5
###### Heading level 6
```

Levels 1 through 6 are valid. Seven or more `#` characters is @ec[B0301]
*"heading level exceeds maximum of 6"*. Missing the space after the
`#` run is @ec[B0302].

## The strict rules

- Exactly one space between the `#` run and the heading text.
- Setext-style headings (`===` and `---` underlines) are *not* supported.
- Multiple spaces, zero spaces, or no text after the `#` run are all
  errors.
- Heading text is parsed as inline content — emphasis, inline code, and
  inline shortcodes work inside it.

@callout(kind: note)
The "monotonic heading levels" rule is configurable. Set
`compile.strict_heading_levels = true` in `brief.toml` to forbid skipping
levels (e.g., going straight from `##` to `####`).
@end

## Heading ids

Every heading gets an id, which is what `#fragment` links and `@ref`
section links point at. By default the id is derived from the heading
text the way GitHub does it: lowercased, with every character that is not
a letter, digit, space, `-` or `_` removed, and spaces turned into `-`.

```brief
## What's *new* in 0.4
```

gets the id `whats-new-in-04`. A heading whose text leaves nothing behind
(only punctuation, say) gets the id `section`.

To pin an id so links survive edits to the heading text, end the line
with an explicit anchor:

```brief
## My section {#my-anchor}
```

Rules:

- The anchor is optional.
- If present, it must be the last thing on the line.
- The identifier matches `[a-z0-9-]+`.
- Exactly one space between the heading text and the `{`.

Ids, automatic or explicit, must be unique within the document: two
headings with the same id are a compile error (@ec[B0506]). Pin one of
them with a distinct `{#id}`.

Links are checked against these ids. `@link[text](#id)` must name a
heading in the same document (@ec[B0606]), and `@ref[file.brf#id](text)`
must name one in the target file — see
@ref[language/shortcodes.brf](Shortcodes) for the syntax.

## Inline content inside headings

```brief
## The `printf` function and *why it matters*
```

Renders the same way it would in a paragraph: `printf` becomes inline code,
"why it matters" becomes bold.

## In LLM mode

Heading sigils are preserved. LLMs use them as structural cues, and
stripping them would lose hierarchy without saving useful tokens. Anchors
are stripped — they are structural metadata, not content.
